<section class="content">

<h2>Notes for the Catechist</h2>

<p><strong>1. This is a delicate topic.</strong> Many students struggle with scruples or, conversely, with a lax conscience. Be sensitive. Don't give the impression that conscience is just "whatever you feel." But also don't crush students who are genuinely trying to do right.</p>

<p><strong>2. Conscience is not a feeling.</strong> This is crucial. Many young people think conscience is "I feel bad about this, so it must be wrong." Feelings are unreliable. Conscience is a judgment of reason, applying moral principles to concrete situations. Help students distinguish between conscience (a judgment) and feelings (an emotion).</p>

<p><strong>3. Address the "conscience vs. authority" tension.</strong> This is where many students struggle. "If conscience is supreme, why do I have to obey the Church?" The answer is in the lesson: conscience is supreme in the subjective order (you must follow your certain conscience) but subordinate in the objective order (it must be formed by truth). This balance is the genius of Catholic moral teaching.</p>

<p><strong>4. Scrupulosity is real.</strong> If you have students who are tormented by guilt over things that are not sins, they need gentle spiritual direction, not more rules. The Ignatian distinction between "consolation" and "desolation" can be helpful here.</p>

<p><strong>5. The witnesses are powerful.</strong> St. Thomas More, St. John Henry Newman, the Confessing Church — these are not abstract ideas. These are people who suffered for conscience. Make them real.</p>

<p><strong>6. Practical formation.</strong> Don't just teach <em>about</em> conscience; teach students <em>to form</em> their conscience. Give them concrete tools: the examination of conscience, the Catechism, spiritual direction.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Adaptation by Age</h2>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌱 Ages 6-8 &mdash; Seed</h4>
<p>Keep it simple: God put a voice inside you that tells you right from wrong. It's like a compass that points to Jesus. Sometimes we listen, sometimes we don't. When we listen, we feel peaceful. When we don't, we feel uncomfortable. That's God speaking to you.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌿 Ages 9-11 &mdash; Sprouts</h4>
<p>Introduce the idea that conscience needs to be "fed" — by the Bible, by what the Church teaches, by prayer. Use the compass analogy: a compass points north, but if there are magnets around it, it can give a wrong reading. Our conscience needs to be protected and calibrated.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🪴 Ages 12-14 &mdash; Root</h4>
<p>Full lesson as written. Discuss types of conscience (lax vs. scrupulous). Address the "my conscience says it's okay" objection. Introduce invincible vs. vincible ignorance. Connect to daily decisions and social pressure.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌳 Ages 15+ &mdash; Tree</4>
<p>Deep theological exploration. The history of conscience in philosophy (Socrates, Kant, Newman). The relationship between conscience and natural law. Contemporary debates: "conscience clauses" in healthcare, the limits of conscience claims. Cardinal Newman's <em>Grammar of Assent</em> and <em>Letter to the Duke of Norfolk</em>.</p>
</div>

</section>

