<section class="content">

<h2>Difficult Questions</h2>

<p><strong>Q: "What if my conscience says something different from the Church?"</strong><br/>
A: First, examine whether your conscience is well-formed. Have you prayed? Have you studied the Church's teaching? Have you sought counsel? Conscience is supreme in the subjective order — you must follow your certain conscience — but it must be formed by objective truth. If after all that, your conscience still differs, you must follow your conscience — but be open to the possibility that your conscience needs further formation.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What if I'm scrupulous and tormented by guilt?"</strong><br/>
A: Scrupulosity is a real struggle. It can be caused by anxiety, OCD, or poor formation. The key is to find a spiritual director who can help you distinguish between real sin and imagined sin. Sometimes the best thing is to "obey your confessor" even when your scruples say otherwise. This is not blind obedience — it's trusting that another's clearer judgment can help heal your conscience.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What about people who sincerely believe evil is good?"</strong><br/>
A: This is invincible ignorance — they genuinely cannot see that it's wrong. They are not culpable for the sin itself, but they may be culpable for not having formed their conscience better. God judges the heart. Our job is to lovingly help people see the truth, not to condemn them.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "Can my conscience change?"</strong><br/>
A: Yes, and it should — through formation. As we grow in virtue and knowledge, our conscience becomes more refined. What once seemed gray becomes clear. This is the work of a lifetime. Don't expect to have perfect clarity on everything immediately.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "Is conscience the same as the Holy Spirit?"</strong><br/>
A: No, but they are related. Conscience is a faculty of the human person, part of our nature as rational beings. The Holy Spirit can work through conscience, illuminating it and strengthening it. But conscience is not infallible — the Holy Spirit is.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Prayer</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, you are the Truth in whom alone we find perfect freedom. Form my conscience by your Word and your Church. Give me the wisdom to teach others not just about conscience but to form their own. Help me be gentle with the scrupulous, clear with the lax, and faithful to the truth in all things.</p>

<p>
St. Thomas More, patron of conscience, pray for us.<br/>
St. John Henry Newman, guardian of truth, pray for us.</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

</section>

